# Make a reading kit for your team

![Quiet worktable with a notebook, pencil, and lamp](assets/67e28b4942b3731bd6bde60d49aa6f064e4711e9ed8ee4cfffa382df22add0a5.webp)

A workshop handout can stay a readable document while adapting the practical
instructions to each participant. Choose the computer you will use, then follow
the matching path. This kit is fictional and works entirely with local files.

@choice platform = "macOS" (
  options: ["macOS", "Windows", "Linux"],
  label: "Your platform",
)
@toggle more_detail = false (label: "Show additional context")

## First, open your file

@when platform == "macOS"
Open the local HTML file in a browser from Finder.

- Keep the source `.tinta` separately for editing.
- A `.tinta.zip` keeps local images with the source.
@end

@when platform == "Windows"
Open the local HTML file in a browser from File Explorer.

The reader does not require an account or a local application server.
@end

@when platform == "Linux"
Open the local HTML file using your browser's **Open file** action.

```text
This code block is descriptive only. Nothing here executes.
```
@end

## Then try the handout

- [ ] Change the platform above and read the new instructions.
- [ ] Turn on additional context when you want to know what an export contains.
- [ ] Open the explanation below only if you need it.

@details "Why keep the source?"
The HTML copy is for reading. The `.tinta` source is the document you can edit,
and a `.tinta.zip` keeps the source together with local images.
@end

@when more_detail
### What is inside the file?

An interactive export contains all source data and hidden explanations.
A static export contains the resolved visible document instead.

@details "Why the distinction matters"
Hiding an explanation in an interactive file does not make it confidential.
@end
@end

Your choice is **{{ platform }}**.
